WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.942
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:16.853
Unit thirty-nine: Review: C1 Grammar. Today you will review the key C1 structures for essays and speeches.

4
00:00:17.153 --> 00:00:20.108
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.241
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.073
Inversion.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:30.105
Inversion means verb before subject for emphasis.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:32.368
Never have I seen it.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:35.229
Your turn. Say: Inversion.

10
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.091
Inversion.

11
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.292
Cleft sentences.

12
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:45.839
Cleft sentences means sentences that focus information.

13
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:47.881
It was Tom who called.

14
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:51.855
Your turn. Say: Cleft sentences.

15
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.398
Cleft sentences.

16
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.209
Subjunctive.

17
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:01.576
Subjunctive means base form for demands.

18
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:03.486
I suggest he go.

19
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.863
Your turn. Say: Subjunctive.

20
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.748
Subjunctive.

21
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:12.914
Participle clauses.

22
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:17.205
Participle clauses means shortened clauses with -ing or -ed.

23
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:19.398
Having left, she called.

24
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:23.147
Your turn. Say: Participle clauses.

25
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.733
Participle clauses.

26
00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:29.206
Fronting.

27
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:32.623
Fronting means moving a word to the front.

28
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:34.856
Into the room walked she.

29
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.917
Your turn. Say: Fronting.

30
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:41.594
Fronting.

31
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.525
Ellipsis.

32
00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:47.132
Ellipsis means omitting understood words.

33
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:50.111
I can, and so can she.

34
00:01:50.717 --> 00:01:53.334
Your turn. Say: Ellipsis.

35
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.387
Ellipsis.

36
00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.290
Modal perfect.

37
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:03.548
Modal perfect means modal + have + participle.

38
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:05.407
She must have left.

39
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:09.099
Your turn. Say: Modal perfect.

40
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:13.201
Modal perfect.

41
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:14.998
Emphasis.

42
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.679
Emphasis means special stress on an idea.

43
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:21.039
Emphasis through structure.

44
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.558
Your turn. Say: Emphasis.

45
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.552
Emphasis.

46
00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:32.959
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:33.817 --> 00:02:37.267
Visal, which C1 structure will you use in your next essay?

48
00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:48.663
I will use a cleft sentence to focus my main point, and a participle clause to keep my sentences concise. Inversion can mark an important contrast.

49
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.900
Which ones belong to formal writing?

50
00:02:53.017 --> 00:03:04.156
The subjunctive, inverted conditionals, passive reporting structures and participle clauses are all formal. Modal perfects and ellipsis appear in both speech and writing.

51
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.866
How do we avoid overuse?

52
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:19.530
Use one or two advanced structures per paragraph, and only where they add meaning. Variety with accuracy is better than complexity for its own sake.

53
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.492
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:31.073
Grammar focus: C1 review: inversion, cleft, subjunctive, participle, fronting, ellipsis, modal perfect.

55
00:03:31.373 --> 00:03:32.479
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.183 --> 00:03:36.646
Inversion adds emphasis: never have I seen it.

57
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.105
A cleft sentence adds focus: it was Tom who called.

58
00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:45.885
A participle clause adds concision: having left, she called.

59
00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:57.032
Mix a few advanced structures carefully: inversion, clefts, the subjunctive, participle clauses, fronting, ellipsis and modal perfects. Accuracy comes first.

60
00:03:57.783 --> 00:04:01.411
Be careful! Don't say: Never I have seen it.

61
00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.506
Say: Never have I seen it.

62
00:04:05.806 --> 00:04:07.201
Invert after never.

63
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.861
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.260
Writing at C1 Level.

65
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.639
Never have I seen such a clear example of good grammar.

66
00:04:17.633 --> 00:04:19.888
It was practice that made the difference.

67
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.794
I suggest that every student review these structures.

68
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.612
Having studied them, you can use them with confidence.

69
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.552
Accuracy and variety together create strong writing.

70
00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:36.599
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.032
Never blank I seen it.

72
00:04:39.332 --> 00:04:42.370
A, have. B, has. C, did.

73
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:47.689
The answer is A: have.

74
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:50.443
It blank Tom who called.

75
00:04:50.743 --> 00:04:53.897
A, was. B, did. C, has.

76
00:04:57.433 --> 00:04:59.537
The answer is A: was.

77
00:05:00.683 --> 00:05:02.787
I suggest that he blank early.

78
00:05:03.087 --> 00:05:06.091
A, leaves. B, leave. C, left.

79
00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:11.610
The answer is B: leave.

80
00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.494
blank the report, she left.

81
00:05:14.794 --> 00:05:18.645
A, Having finished. B, Finish. C, To finished.

82
00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.525
The answer is A: Having finished.

83
00:05:25.683 --> 00:05:28.240
According to the reading, what made the difference?

84
00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.996
A, practice. B, luck. C, money.

85
00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:37.579
The answer is A: practice.

86
00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:41.849
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:45.809
Which C1 structure will you use in your next essay?

88
00:05:54.617 --> 00:05:56.140
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:10.310
I will use a cleft sentence to focus my main idea, and an inverted conditional to make my writing more formal. I will also use a participle clause to keep one sentence short. I will check each structure for accuracy.

90
00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.424
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:22.529
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and c1 review: inversion, cleft, subjunctive, participle, fronting, ellipsis, modal perfect.

92
00:06:22.829 --> 00:06:26.793
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

93
00:06:27.093 --> 00:06:33.521
Congratulations! You have finished Online Grammar, level C one. See you in Online Grammar, level C two!

94
00:06:33.821 --> 00:06:36.764
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

